  "comments": "<p>Despite being listed in the \"Standard Catalog of World Coins\" and Passon as bronze, official US Mint records state that these coins were minted in Copper and Zinc (Brass).<br><br>1944 coin:<br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5fd3fa73351b4.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5fd3fa73351b4.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Palm tree: mark of the coins which were struck for the overseas territories of the Kingdom during WWII<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Mint mark: D, Denver, United States of America<br>&nbsp;</li></ul><p><br>1947/1948 coins:<br>&nbsp;</p><ul><li>Mint mark: caduceus, Koninklijke Nederlandse Munt (Royal Dutch Mint), Utrecht, the Netherlands<br>&nbsp;</li><li>Privy mark: fish - Dr J.W.A. van den Hengel<br>&nbsp;</li></ul><p><br>Two main groups of the reverse are known.<br><br><i>Reverse</i><br>Die <strong>A</strong> - bold letters, wide ribbon.<br>Die <strong>B</strong> - fine letters, narrow ribbon.<br><strong>Note</strong>. Many differences in wreath additionally shown on the picture.<br>Picture: © Images courtesy of Alexandr Prokofyev.<br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/61d641a3008b6.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/61d641a3008b6.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br><br><i>Combinations</i><br><strong>A</strong>: 1944.<br><strong>B</strong>: 1947, 1948.<br><br>According to Passon (2022) 1944 varieties exist:<br>- Without mint mark D<br>- Without mint mark D, aluminium<br>- Off metal strike: Silver plated card board (1 piece)<br>- Off metal strike: Lead (1 piece)<br>- Off metal strike: Lead; without mint mark D (1 piece)</p>",
